---
// src/sections/Story.astro
// THE EDITORIAL that explains the descent graph, in two columns on a paper ground.
//
// The owner's brief: "after the descent line, a section explaining ourselves, incl. why we transition
// into quants… the section explains the graph literally. You maybe even can get creative, try 2
// columns etc." And the register: the earlier "forty-two sentences" sketch read like a good journal
// spread, but its CONTENT was photograph notes — "after all we are not an artist". So this keeps that
// typographic register and puts the actual subject in it.
//
// WHY A PAPER GROUND. The rest of the homepage is a sky you descend through. This section is the one
// place that stops and talks, so it gets a different surface: an editorial paper panel, full-bleed,
// with the sky visible above and below it. That is also the elegant thing the owner picked out about
// the light theme, and the panel colour is chosen per theme rather than shared.
//
// Every number here is COMPUTED at build time from the same field the graph draws, so the prose and
// the picture cannot drift apart. Nothing is hand-typed.
//
// THE PANEL NOW WRAPS THE GRAPH TOO. The owner: "in the descent one climb section, you might want to
// completely turn off the fluid background and use the paper editorial background." That is one
// slide — the picture and the words that read it — so it is one surface, and DescentPath renders as
// a <slot> inside this panel rather than as a sibling floating on the sky. Turning the fluid off
// behind it is done by the panel being opaque, not by touching the fluid layer: the sky keeps
// running above and below, and this slide simply sits on paper.
// The figure row is deleted (see below), so the measurement imports go with it — curvature(),
// discoveryOf() and trajectoryFacts() were only ever called here to fill those three numbers. The
// modules stay: lib/sensitivity and lib/knowledge are what the GRAPH is drawn from, and they keep their
// own specs.
import { STORY } from '../data/story';
---
<section id="story" class="story" aria-labelledby="story-heading">
  <div class="story-paper">
    <!-- ONE SECTION, TWO COLUMNS. The owner: "I'm thinking about if it's more natural to merge A
         descent with one climb with Why the climb. After all they are all describing my career path.
         There is also a huge right panel unused here."
         Both are right. They were two stacked slides describing one subject, and at a wide viewport the
         graph left 586px — 29.3% of the window — genuinely empty on the right, because the field's
         aspect (1.27) cannot stretch to fill a letterbox frame and the surplus became padding.
         So the prose moves INTO that space: graph left, writing right, one section. The graph is
         STICKY, so it stays visible while its explanation scrolls past — the whole point of merging is
         that you can read the claim and check the picture at the same time. -->
    <div class="story-split">
      <!-- The graph, on the same paper. Passed in as a slot so this component owns the surface and the
           ink tokens, and DescentPath stays a self-contained piece that inherits them. -->
      <div class="story-figure-slot"><slot /></div>

      <div class="story-inner">
        <h2 id="story-heading" class="story-title">Why the climb</h2>

        <div class="story-cols">
          {STORY.map((col) => (
            <div class="story-col">
              <p class="story-kicker">{col.kicker}</p>
              <p class="story-lead">{col.lead}</p>
              {col.body.map((para) => <p class="story-body">{para}</p>)}
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <!-- THE FIGURES ARE GONE. They were computed at build time rather than typed, but computed from
             the HERO'S INVENTED FIELD — so "260x feedback ratio" was an exact measurement of a fiction,
             printed where a reader would take it as a fact about a career. A true number about a made-up
             surface is worse than no number, because it borrows the authority of measurement.

             THE CODA MOVED INTO THE GRAPH (see DescentPath), because this column is taller than one screen:
             the deck gesture that should scroll down to its last line advances to the next slide instead, so
             anything at the foot of this column is unreadable by normal scrolling.

             The replacement tail line I put here had EXACTLY THE SAME BUG — same column, same fold, same
             unreachable position — so it is deleted rather than restyled. The section now ends with its last
             body paragraph, and the coda in the drawing is the closing line. Nothing goes at the bottom of
             this column again. -->
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Full-bleed, so the paper panel is a surface the page passes through rather than a card sitting
     on the sky. No vertical padding: the panel itself must reach the section's edges, or a band of
     sky shows above and below it and the "turn the fluid off here" reads as a floating card.
     z-index lifts it over the SkyWash and .descent::after washes (both z-index:-1), so the fluid is
     completely hidden behind this slide rather than tinting the paper. */
  .story {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    width: 100vw;
    margin-left: calc(50% - 50vw);
    /* NO LEAD ON THIS SECTION. The lead lifts the deck's resting position ABOVE the element, which for a
       full-bleed opaque panel means a strip of sky shows above the paper — measured: paper starts at
       1953, the deck rested at 1893, so 60px of the descent sky sat above the panel edge and the slide
       read as a card that had slipped down.
       The air the title needs is the panel's own padding-top instead, which is inside the paper. */
  }

  /* THE PAPER GROUND. Two different colours by theme rather than one shared value:
     - light: a warm bone/oat that reads as printed stock against the luminous sky, NOT the same
       --paper as the text colour (which would vanish);
     - dark: a cool near-black slate a shade above --bg, so the panel is felt rather than seen. */
  .story-paper {
    /* DARKER THAN THE FIRST DRAFT, and this was measured rather than eyeballed. The owner: "in the
       light theme it might be better to have a darker background for the two bottom tabs, because when
       you pass the tagline the fluid is already mildly warm-dark, so getting back to full light is a
       sharp contrast."
       Sampled at the seam, light theme: the sky just above reads rgb(115,115,123), luminance 0.173,
       while the old #efe7d8 panel was 0.805 — a 4.5x jump at the boundary, which is exactly the snap
       back to full light he describes. This tone (0.482) more than halves it to 2.4x while keeping the
       ink at 8.7:1, far above the 4.5:1 floor. A toned printing stock rather than bright paper. */
    --story-bg: #c4b7a1;
    --story-edge: rgba(74, 66, 54, 0.26);
    --story-ink: #1f1b14;
    --story-ink-soft: #423b30;
    --story-ink-faint: #6a6153;
    --story-accent: #7c5522;
    /* The graph's own ink, dark-on-paper. The canvas reads these (see DescentPath) because its
       default tokens — --paper, --ink-3..5 — are LIGHT inks chosen for the dark sky, and on a light
       paper ground they render invisible. Same reason the panel can't reuse --paper as a background. */
    /* Darker than before to hold up on the toned ground: the old values were chosen against a near-white
       panel, and a 0.72-alpha mid-grey on 0.48 luminance stock loses the faint contour dots. */
    --story-line: rgba(46, 40, 31, 0.8);
    --story-line-soft: rgba(72, 64, 52, 0.7);

    background: var(--story-bg);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--story-edge);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--story-edge);
    padding: 9vh 0;
  }
  :global(html[data-theme='dark']) .story-paper {
    --story-bg: #14161a;
    --story-edge: rgba(184, 177, 161, 0.14);
    --story-ink: #dce1dc;
    --story-ink-soft: #a8b0a9;
    --story-ink-faint: #6d746e;
    --story-accent: #7fc9a0;
    --story-line: rgba(214, 220, 214, 0.66);
    --story-line-soft: rgba(160, 168, 161, 0.5);
  }

  /* ── THE SPLIT: graph left, writing right, in one section.
     64% to the graph, measured rather than guessed: at 2000px that gives the drawing 1091px, slightly
     WIDER than the 1004px it occupied when it had the whole slide (the surplus was padding the aspect
     guard could not use), while leaving the prose a real 720px panel. */
  .story-split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 64fr 36fr;
    gap: 0 clamp(28px, 3vw, 64px);
    align-items: start;
    /* Clears the fixed Toc rail, which spans x=34..129 at 1700px — the previous clamp resolved to 128px
       and the rail's labels sat on the graph's own header. Shared with the other paper slides. */
    /* Clears the fixed Toc rail. The rail now carries an INDENTED tier ("Problem / Limits / Method" nested
       under "The field"), so its right edge moved from x=129 to x=151 at 1700px and the old clamp left as
       little as 5px of air — measured at 1440px on this slide. 168px min / 10.4vw keeps at least ~20px of
       margin at every width the rail is shown (it collapses to dots below 1100px). Shared across the five
       paper slides, so it is edited in all of them together or in none. */
    padding-left: clamp(168px, 10.4vw, 200px);
    padding-right: clamp(20px, 2.4vw, 52px);
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }
  @media (max-width: 1099px) {
    .story-split { padding-left: clamp(28px, 6vw, 80px); }
  }

  .story-inner { width: 100%; margin: 0; max-width: none; }

  /* Below the split's comfortable width the two columns stack: a 36% prose column becomes unreadable
     long before the graph stops being legible, and the graph's in-frame labels need room. */
  @media (max-width: 1180px) {
    .story-split { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 8vh 0; }
  }

  /* STICKY, so the drawing stays beside its explanation while the prose scrolls. That is the argument
     for merging in the first place: the reader can check each claim against the picture without
     scrolling back to it. Sticky is bounded by its parent, so the graph releases by itself at the end
     of the writing — no JS, no scroll listener.
     No --deck-lead here: the slot is no longer a deck stop (the deck's slides are top-level sections),
     and leaving one would inherit down and re-open the sky gap above the panel. */
  .story-figure-slot {
    position: sticky;
    top: clamp(40px, 6vh, 80px);
    width: 100%;
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }
  /* ── AND IT STOPS STICKING ONCE THE SPLIT STACKS. Sticky is what keeps the graph beside its explanation, and
     that only means anything while there IS a column beside it. Stacked, the figure sits ABOVE the prose, so
     pinning it makes it ride down OVER the writing: measured at 390x844, a 744px-tall figure pinned at
     top: 50.6px, printing its heading and replay button across the paragraphs at two of the three phone deck
     stops. The rule was written for the two-column layout and was never bounded to it.
     PLACED AFTER THE BASE RULE ON PURPOSE. My first attempt put this inside the max-width: 1180px block above,
     which sits BEFORE this declaration — same specificity, so source order won and the override did nothing
     (measured: still position: sticky at 320 and 390). The same trap this file's own notes describe for the
     Toc, and it is silent every time. */
  @media (max-width: 1180px) {
    .story-figure-slot { position: static; top: auto; }
  }
  /* The horizontal insets live on .story-split now, so both columns share one left edge and the rail is
     cleared once rather than per-column. */

  /* The "why the climb" cue is GONE — it existed because the writing was a separate slide below the
     graph, and an unmarked bottom edge read as the end of the page. The writing is now beside the graph
     in the same section, so there is nothing below to announce. */

  .story-title {
    font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 300;
    font-size: clamp(28px, 2.4vw, 42px);
    color: var(--story-ink); margin: 0 0 clamp(24px, 3.4vh, 46px);
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* ONE COLUMN NOW, not two. The piece is still a comparison — where I was against where the signal
     was — but in a 36%-wide panel two sub-columns would be ~330px each, far under a readable measure.
     The pairing is carried by the two kickers and a rule between the blocks instead of by side-by-side
     geometry: same contrast, read top-to-bottom. */
  .story-cols { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0; }
  .story-col + .story-col {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--story-edge);
    margin-top: clamp(24px, 3vh, 40px);
    padding-top: clamp(24px, 3vh, 40px);
  }
  /* Stacked (narrow) layout: the prose has the full width back, so the two blocks can sit side by side
     again — which is where the comparison reads best. */
  @media (max-width: 1180px) and (min-width: 861px) {
    .story-cols { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 0 clamp(32px, 5vw, 88px); }
    .story-col + .story-col {
      border-top: 0; margin-top: 0; padding-top: 0;
      border-left: 1px solid var(--story-edge);
      padding-left: clamp(32px, 5vw, 88px);
    }
  }

  .story-kicker {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 11.5px; letter-spacing: 0.24em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--story-accent); margin: 0 0 18px;
  }
  /* ── TYPE in the proto-sketches register, which is the style the owner preferred: that sketch pulled
     its focal sentences out at 34px on a 44px line (lib/sketches/batch1.ts — the "forty-two sentences"
     frame, the only one that measured as not reading empty).
     Sized in ch/rem terms against the PROSE COLUMN rather than the viewport, because the column is now
     36% of the window: a 2.3vw lead measured 46px at 2000px wide and would have been ~4 words per line
     in a 720px panel. cqw units would be ideal but container queries would need a container declared on
     an element the deck also measures, so this stays on a viewport clamp with a tighter cap. */
  .story-lead {
    font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: clamp(21px, 1.7vw, 30px);
    line-height: 1.34; color: var(--story-ink); margin: 0 0 22px;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .story-body {
    font-size: clamp(15px, 0.95vw, 17px); line-height: 1.74; color: var(--story-ink-soft);
    margin: 0 0 16px; max-width: 46ch;
  }
  .story-body:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  /* The figures: measured readings, mono, so they read as instrument output rather than pull-quotes.
     Three across needed ~1300px; in the 720px prose panel they wrap to a responsive grid instead of
     being crushed to 220px each. */
  .story-figures {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(180px, 1fr));
    gap: clamp(20px, 2.4vh, 32px) clamp(20px, 2vw, 40px);
    margin: clamp(28px, 4vh, 52px) 0 0; padding-top: clamp(22px, 3vh, 40px);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--story-edge);
  }
  .story-figure dt {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: clamp(26px, 3vw, 40px);
    color: var(--story-accent); line-height: 1;
  }
  .story-figure dd {
    margin: 14px 0 0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--story-ink-faint);
  }
  .story-figure-label {
    display: block; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--story-ink-soft); margin-bottom: 7px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .story-figures { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 4vh 0; }
  }

  /* (.story-tail is gone with the element — see the markup comment. Anything placed at the foot of this
     column is unreachable in the deck, so the rule is deleted rather than left for someone to reuse.) */
</style>
